e 1948. Ontario's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.03\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.23 to +0.16) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1985, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+1.45\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +1.08 to +1.81) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.50\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.03 to +0.97). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 9 of 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Ontario's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.01 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 8.9 inches a year. The wettest year was 1983 (16.89 in); the driest, 1949 (4.86 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 4 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}